A child asking for a share of a Compose Row is weight(), and CSS stretches a
flex child across the row too, which fillMaxHeight() stood in for. But
fillMaxHeight() fills the most the row may be, not its tallest child, so a
bar in a stats row centered by `alignItems` took the whole screen.
A row whose Style names an alignment other than `stretch` gives the share
alone; one that names none still stretches.

-- m.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

ui.window {
    ui.vbox {
        ui.hbox(style={alignItems=center}) {
            ui.text(value="HP", style={width=120px})
            ui.progress(value=45.0, max=255.0, style={flex=1})
        }
        ui.hbox {
            ui.button(text="7", style={flex=1})
            ui.button(text="8", style={flex=1})
        }
    }
}
